.TH OPENCODE-SESSION-RECOVER 1 "2026-09-30" "opencode-session-recover" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
opencode-session-recover \- repair opencode v1 to v2 session visibility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B opencode-session-recover
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B opencode-session-recover
repairs the session history database used by
.BR opencode (1)
after a V1 to V2 upgrade. The one\-time V1 to V2 history migration copies rows
from the legacy
.IR session ", " message " and " part
tables into
.IR session_v2 " and " session_message .
It is gated by the
.B migration.v1\-v2
key in the
.B kv
table and runs once.
.P
Two problems follow from that design. First, sessions created in V1 after the
migration completed are never copied. Second, every copied V1 session keeps the
legacy project id
.IR global ,
which V2 does not list for a real directory, so old conversations are hidden
even though they are present.
.B opencode-session-recover
fixes both: it re\-runs the migration idempotently for any session still
missing, then re\-files legacy
.I global
sessions under the project V2 now uses for their directory.
.P
The legacy
.IR session ", " message " and " part
tables are never modified, so they remain a fallback copy. The database lives
under
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/opencode/ ,
or
.I ~/.local/share/opencode/
when unset.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Operate on a throwaway copy of the database and change nothing. The copy is
snapshotted with
.BR sqlite3 (1)
.B VACUUM
.BR INTO ,
an isolated server runs the migration against it, and the resulting counts and
newly imported sessions are printed.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Do not prompt for confirmation before modifying the live database.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Operate on
.I PATH
instead of the default database. Overrides
.BR OPENCODE_DB .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS What it does
.IP \(bu 2
Counts sessions present in the legacy
.I session
table but missing from
.IR session_v2 .
.IP \(bu 2
If any are missing, stops the OpenCode service, snapshots the database, sets
.B migration.v1\-v2
to
.B {"phase":"sessions"}
and starts the service so its boot migration imports them. The value is set
rather than deleted on purpose: an undefined phase also clears the
.I event
table.
.IP \(bu 2
Stops the service again and updates
.I session_v2.project_id
from
.I global
to the project whose worktree matches each session's directory; rows with no
matching project are left untouched.
.IP \(bu 2
Restarts the service and prints per\-metric counts plus the sessions imported by
that run.
.PP
If nothing is missing and nothing needs re\-filing, the script reports that the
database is already consistent and exits without touching it.
.SS Requirements
.IP \(bu 2
.BR sqlite3 (1)
must be installed.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR opencode (1)
must be on
.BR PATH .
.IP \(bu 2
Close every OpenCode terminal and desktop client before a real run: the script
stops and restarts the shared background service, which disconnects them.
.SS Environment
.TP
.B OPENCODE_DB
Default database path when
.B \-\-db
is not given.
.TP
.B OPENCODE_RECOVER_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for the migration to report completion. Default:
.IR 300 .
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview the repair without changing anything:
.IP
.EX
opencode-session-recover \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.P
Apply the repair without a confirmation prompt:
.IP
.EX
opencode-session-recover \-\-yes
.EE
.P
Operate on a specific database:
.IP
.EX
opencode-session-recover \-\-db /tmp/opencode/opencode.db
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or nothing was left to do.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: bad options, missing database, missing dependency, or a failed backup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db
Default OpenCode database.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db.bak\- YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS
Consistent snapshot written before any modification. Keep it until the repaired
database has been verified, then remove it by hand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sqlite3 (1),
.BR dotfiles-sync (1)
